version: 2
title: How a version-1 policy works
description: One extension, six documents — the bucket machine, a run over a list, a tags policy, an order policy, a table policy, and a ranking — how the file's role is picked, what each role draws, what each click does, and where the same rules are applied from elsewhere.
decisions:
  - key: role
    label: Which role does the file play?
    detail: Picked from the text before anything is drawn — see "How the role is picked" under Always.
    values:
      - key: bucket
        label: The bucket machine
        detail: No `role`, no `policy`+`items` — params, an ordered switch of cases, buckets. The default.
      - key: run
        label: A run
        detail: "`policy:` and `items:` — the policy applied to every row of a list."
      - key: tags
        label: A tags policy
        detail: "`role: tags` — dimensions with derive rules; stage one of the chain."
      - key: order
        label: An order policy
        detail: "`role: order` — a ranking over tag combinations; stage two of the chain."
      - key: table
        label: A table policy
        detail: "`role: table` — rules over data rows, applied by a `.jsonl`."
      - key: ranking
        label: A ranking
        detail: "`decisions:` and `items:` at the top level — buckets that fall out of answers, with hand tags written back."
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — how the role is picked, the shape of each, the one clause vocabulary, and where the engines live.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shapes of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shapes of the file
        description: Six documents share the extension; the text decides which one it is.
        sections:
          - title: How the role is picked
            body: |
              In this order, before anything is drawn:

              1. A line `decisions:` AND a line `items:` at column 0 → a RANKING (`parseRanking`).
              2. `role: tags` (or `role: decisions`) → a tags policy; `role: order` → an order policy;
                 `role: table` → a table policy (`parsePolicyKindFile`).
              3. `policy:` and `items:` both present → a run (`parsePolicyFile`).
              4. Anything else → the bucket machine (`parsePolicy`).

              The parser never throws in any branch. The checker takes the table branch apart
              through its own problem list, and the bucket machine and a run through
              `policyProblems`; a tags or order policy passes whenever its parser does.
          - title: The bucket machine
            body: |
              ```yaml
              title: Triage
              params:
                - {key: name, label: Name, type: string}      # string (default) | number | boolean
              buckets:
                - {key: "yes", label: "Yes", start: "09:00", end: "10:30"}   # start/end optional — a timeslot
                - {key: "no", label: "No"}
              default: "no"                                   # omitted = the built-in "unmatched"
              cases:                                          # an ordered SWITCH; first match wins and stops
                - label: The first filter
                  when: [{param: name, op: not_empty}]        # ALL clauses must hold; empty = always
                  bucket: "yes"
              ```

              Deterministic: no dates, no randomness, no IO. A case without a `bucket` is dropped;
              a clause without a `param` or with an unknown `op` is dropped from its case — the
              switch stays strict so it never guesses.
          - title: A run
            body: |
              ```yaml
              title: This week's leads
              policy: order.policy        # a bucket policy, or an order policy (the chain)
              items: leads.list           # the rows
              map: {text: [label, summary], salary: pay}   # param → list column(s); "label" is the row's label
              date: 2026-09-19            # the day the run stands at (the calendar face)
              ```
          - title: A tags policy
            body: |
              ```yaml
              role: tags
              title: Lead facts
              params: [{key: text, type: string}]
              tags:                                   # `decisions:` reads the same
                - key: platform
                  label: Platform
                  values: [ios, android, {key: other, label: Other}]
                  derive:                             # ordered; first match tags; no `when` = the default
                    - {value: ios, when: [{param: text, op: contains, value: [swift, ios]}]}
                    - {value: other}
                - key: fit
                  label: Fit
                  values: [good, poor]
                  from:                               # COMPUTED from dimensions ABOVE it — refs, or a mapping
                    - {value: good, when: {platform: ios}}
                    - {value: poor}
                  hidden: true                        # plumbing: tags, but Input panes skip it
              ```

              A clause in `derive` without an `op` means `contains`. A `from` mapping value may be
              a list — `{seniority: [senior, unstated]}` — the value-OR ref `seniority=senior|unstated`.
          - title: An order policy
            body: |
              ```yaml
              role: order
              title: What to chase first
              tags: facts.policy          # the tags policy it consumes, relative to this file
              order:                      # best first; position IS rank; first match wins
                - {label: iOS, when: {platform: ios, fit: good}}
                - {when: [platform=android]}
                - {}                      # no refs: everything left — least priority IS excluded
              ```

              There is no veto lane. Items nothing claims are UNRANKED — parked, never discarded —
              which only happens without a catch-all entry.
          - title: A table policy
            body: |
              ```yaml
              role: table
              title: Within the price band, cheapest first      # a name for the RULES, never the data
              where:
                - {field: available_for_dates, op: is_true}
                - {field: best_offer.price_total_aud, op: between, value: [600, 2000]}
              sort: [{field: best_offer.price_total_aud, dir: asc}]
              columns: [name, sleeps, best_offer.price_total_aud, "*"]
              hide: [images]
              limit: 100
              ```

              It knows nothing about the data: `source` and `rename` are refused, since the
              `.jsonl` that applies it names the sources and the header labels.
          - title: A ranking
            body: |
              ```yaml
              title: Leads, ranked
              items: leads.list
              map: {text: [label, summary]}
              decisions:
                - key: platform
                  label: Platform
                  values: [ios, other]
                  derive: [{value: ios, when: [{param: text, op: contains, value: swift}]}, {value: other}]
              ranking:                    # priority order; a group is a predicate over answers
                - {label: iOS roles, when: {platform: ios}}
                - {label: Everything else, when: []}

              # Answers taken by hand. Written by the ranking view — keep last.
              tags:
                "Lead title": [platform=ios]
              ```

              A row's answer to a decision comes from a hand tag first, then a list field named
              like the decision, then the first derive rule that holds; failing all, it is
              UNANSWERED — a real state, not a zero.
          - title: The clause vocabulary
            body: |
              One vocabulary everywhere a clause is written — cases, derive rules, `where`, a
              middleware's rules, a memory's include: `equals`, `not_equals`, `contains`,
              `not_contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `matches` (regex, case-insensitive), `gt`,
              `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `between` (`[low, high]`, inclusive), `in`, `not_in`, `is_empty`,
              `not_empty`, `is_true`, `is_false`. String matching is case-insensitive; a text op
              takes one value or a list (any of them; none of them for the `not_` ops); an empty
              value is unknown, so numeric clauses fail on it; a bad regex simply fails. YAML
              booleans as values read as `yes`/`no`.
          - title: Where the engines live
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/policyDoc.ts` — `parsePolicy`, `parsePolicyFile`,
              `parseClauses`, `clauseHolds`, `applyPolicy`, `bucketOrder`, `inputForRow`,
              `hasTimedBuckets`; `policyChain.ts` — `parseTagsPolicy`, `parseOrderPolicy`,
              `parsePolicyKindFile`, `deriveTags`, `applyOrder`, `whenHolds`; `tablePolicy.ts` —
              `parseTablePolicy`, `applyTablePolicy`, `clauseSentence`, `rulesSummary`;
              `rankingDoc.ts` — `parseRanking`, `answerFor`, `assignmentFor`, `rankOf`,
              `writeTags`. The headers of the first three are what `studio-check --spec policy`
              prints. The view is `components/policy/PolicyView.tsx`, which hands the other roles
              to `PolicyRunView`, `TagsPolicyView`, `OrderPolicyView`, `TablePolicyView`,
              `RankingView` and `DaySchedule`; the checker is `python/studio_kinds/kinds/policy.py`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: The role is picked, the parse never throws, and the role's own view is drawn — a run and a ranking read other files first.
    hint: Say which role the file plays — each is parsed and drawn differently.
    content:
      key: draw
      label: What is parsed and drawn
      kind: md
      by: [role]
      docs:
        role=bucket: |
          **The parse.** `parsePolicy`: the title ("Policy" when absent), the params (a `key` is
          required; `type` other than `number` or `boolean` reads as `string`), the cases (a
          `bucket` is required; each clause needs a `param` and a known `op`, else it is dropped
          from the case), the buckets (a `key` is required; `start`/`end` kept only as `HH:MM`),
          and `default`.

          **What is drawn.** Three panes under the title. "Input parameters": one field per param
          — a checkbox for a boolean, a number box, a multi-line text box — or "No params
          declared.", and "Apply policy". "Switch — first match wins": every case numbered, its
          label (or "case N"), a `→ bucket` chip, and its clauses as `when name not empty` lines
          ("always" for none); under them a dashed "no case matches → <default>" row. "Buckets":
          "Apply the policy to an input — it lands here, grouped by bucket." until something is
          applied. Nothing is read from disk.
        role=run: |
          **The parse.** `parsePolicyFile` sees `policy` and `items`: the title ("Policy run"),
          `map` (a string or a list per param), and `date` only as `YYYY-MM-DD`.

          **The read.** "Applying the policy…" while the policy file (`parsePolicyKindFile`) and
          the list (collated, if it has sources) are read, both resolved against this file's
          folder. A bucket policy: every row is mapped (`label` is the row's label, anything else
          a field, several joined with a space) and sent through the switch. An order policy: the
          chain — the tags policy named by the order is read relative to it, each row is tagged
          (`deriveTags`) and then ranked (`applyOrder`). Any other role: "The run's policy is a
          <role> document — point it at a policy or an order policy." with a Reload chip; an
          unreadable file shows its error the same way.

          **What is drawn.** The header: the title, `N items · <policy title>`, `· tagged by
          <tags title>` for a chain, `· reloaded HH:MM:SS` after a reload; a "Calendar" chip when
          the policy's buckets carry hours; "Rules" (bucket) or "Order policy" (chain) and
          "Reload" chips; on a phone, Items / Buckets toggles. Left: the list exactly as its own
          view renders it. Right: the same rows grouped in the policy's order — the buckets as
          declared, then buckets only cases name, then the default; or the ranks best first, then
          "Unranked" when the order has no catch-all — with a pill per group carrying its count,
          and on each row a chip: `rule N: <label>`, "no rule matched — default", or "nothing
          claimed it — parked". The Calendar face lays the rows at their buckets' hours on the
          run's `date`, with an "Unscheduled" strip for buckets with no times.
        role=tags: |
          **The parse.** `parseTagsPolicy`: `tags` (or `decisions`) → dimensions with a `key`
          (required), label, detail, values (a bare string, or `{key, label, detail}`), `derive`
          rules (a `value` is required; clauses lenient — no `op` means `contains`, an unknown op
          drops the clause), `from` rules (refs, or a mapping; a list value becomes a value-OR
          ref), and `hidden`. The params are read the bucket machine's way.

          **What is drawn.** The header: the title and "tags policy — rules are tested top to
          bottom within each dimension; the first match tags it". One card per dimension: its
          label, one chip per value, "computed" when it has `from` rules ("Computed from the
          answers of the dimensions above it, not from the item's params"), "hidden" when it is
          plumbing, its detail, then the rules numbered — a clause line in monospace (or the refs
          in the dimensions' words for a computed rule; "otherwise (default)" for a rule with
          none) and a `→ value` chip; "No rules — this dimension is never tagged."; "no rule
          matches → untagged" when no rule is a default. Under the cards, "What it reads": the
          params with their types. Nothing is read from disk.
        role=order: |
          **The parse.** `parseOrderPolicy`: the title ("Order"), the `tags` ref, and the
          entries — label, detail, `when` as refs or a mapping (a list value → value-OR).

          **The read.** The tags policy is read relative to this file; without a `tags` ref:
          "This order policy names no `tags:` document."; an unreadable one shows its error.

          **What is drawn.** The header: the title, "order policy — tag combinations ranked,
          position is priority", and a "Tags policy" chip. Left, "Input — tags": "from <tags
          title> — pick values to watch where the combination ranks", then the visible
          dimensions as playbook-style pills. Right, "Output — the order, best first": every
          entry numbered with its label, one chip per ref (`Platform: iOS`, "or" for a value-OR
          ref), and its detail; "anything" for an entry with no refs; "No entries — nothing is
          ranked." when empty; and, only when no entry is a catch-all, a dashed "anything left —
          unranked, parked" row.
        role=table: |
          **The parse.** `parseTablePolicy`, with problems: `where N: no field` / `no op` /
          `unknown op "x"`, `sort N: no field` / `dir must be asc or desc, not "x"`, `rename:
          header labels belong to the .jsonl that applies this policy ($labels), not to the
          rules`, `source: the data is named by the .jsonl that applies this policy ($sources),
          not by the rules`, `limit: must be a positive number`. `field` and `param` both read.

          **What is drawn.** The title (else the file's name, else "Table policy") and
          description; the problems in an amber box; then the rules in words — "Keeps the rows
          where" (one sentence per clause: "price between 600 and 2000", "name contains any of
          “caravan”, “truck”"; or "every row — no filters"), "In the order" (`field` ascending or
          descending; or "as they come from the sources"), "Showing the columns" (the listed
          columns as chips, less the hidden; "every column the rows carry, except the hidden
          ones"; "Hidden: …"; "At most N rows."). The rows are never here: they belong to the
          `.jsonl` that names its sources and this policy.
        role=ranking: |
          **The parse.** `parseRanking`: the title ("Ranking"), `items`, `map`, the decisions
          (`key` required; values bare or `{key, label, detail, activates, when}`; `derive`
          rules with lenient clauses), the groups (`label` required; `when` as refs or a
          mapping), and the `tags` block (row label → refs).

          **The read.** "Working out the answers…" while the list is read, collated if it has
          sources. Every row is mapped like a run's, then answered decision by decision — a hand
          tag, else a list field named like the decision (matched on key or label,
          case-insensitively), else the first derive rule whose clauses hold, else unanswered —
          and the first group whose refs the answers meet claims it; none, and it is unranked.

          **What is drawn.** The header: the title, `N rows · N decisions · N groups`, `· N with
          an open question` when any row has an unanswered decision, and a "Reload" chip. Left:
          the list as its own view. Right: the rows grouped by rank, best group first, "Unranked"
          last, with a pill per group and a per-row line of its answers; or the Decisions panel —
          per decision, every value with how many rows took it, "N tagged · N from the list · N
          by rule", the derive rules in order, and "Used by N groups: …" or "No group names this
          decision — it labels rows but does not rank them."; "No decisions yet — add a
          `decisions:` block and every row gains an answer." when there are none.
  - key: applied
    label: An input is applied
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    when: [role=bucket]
    detail: The form is sent through the switch; the item lands in its bucket; nothing is written.
    content:
      key: apply
      label: What Apply policy does
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The form's values become one input — a boolean from its checkbox, a number from its box
        (empty stays empty, and a numeric clause then fails), text as typed. `applyPolicy` walks
        the cases in order; the first whose clauses all hold wins and the switch stops; none, and
        the item takes `default` (or "unmatched"). The winning case lights up in the middle pane
        (or the dashed "no case matches" row). The item joins the right pane under its bucket's
        heading, labelled with the first string param's value (or "item N") and `case N` or
        "default". Apply again and the items accumulate — the same 1-D grouping a project gets
        for a list run through the policy. "clear" empties them. The file is untouched.
  - key: combination-picked
    label: A combination is picked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    when: [role=order]
    detail: Tag pills build a combination; the order says where it lands.
    content:
      key: pick
      label: What the pills do
      kind: md
      doc: |
        Each pill click takes or drops one answer (one per dimension). With any answer taken,
        `applyOrder` walks the entries top to bottom; the first whose refs all hold — a value-OR
        ref holding when any alternative is taken — "claims it" and is outlined; an entry lower
        down that would also hold reads "also matches" ("the order stops at the first match");
        entries that accept every chosen answer but are not yet satisfied are lightly outlined
        as candidates; with nothing claiming it, the dashed row reads "lands here". "clear" drops
        the answers. The "Tags policy" chip opens the tags policy in a dialog — one level down,
        where each value is derived from an item. Nothing is written.
  - key: value-clicked
    label: A value is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    when: [role=tags]
    detail: The rules that produce that value stay bright; the others dim.
    content:
      key: highlight
      label: What the click does
      kind: md
      doc: |
        "Where does Platform = iOS come from?" is one click: the value's chip turns solid and
        every rule in the card that does not yield it fades, so the ordered rules that produce it
        read on their own. A second click clears it. One value per card; nothing is written.
  - key: run-row-clicked
    label: A grouped row is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    when: [role=run]
    detail: Why it landed there — the tags it took or the case that claimed it, and what the policy saw.
    content:
      key: why
      label: The why dialog, and the header chips
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The dialog names the row and its group, then: for a chain, every dimension's answer with
        how it came to be (the rule's label, its refs in the dimensions' words, "default", or
        "untagged"); for a bucket policy, the case that claimed it with its clauses, or "no rule
        matched"; then "what the policy saw" — the mapped input, param by param — and the row's
        own fields with any link a click away. A row on the calendar face opens the same dialog.

        "Rules" opens the decision table — every case grouped under the bucket it feeds, with how
        many rows each claimed and how many fell to the default; "Order policy" opens the order
        policy's own view in a dialog, and from there the tags policy. "Reload" re-reads the
        policy and the list and applies the rules again, stamping `reloaded HH:MM:SS` in the
        header — the way an edited rule shows up without reopening. Nothing is written.
  - key: ranking-row-clicked
    label: A ranked row is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    when: [role=ranking]
    detail: The row's assignment, as pills — and clicking a pill writes a hand tag into this file.
    content:
      key: tag
      label: The assignment dialog, and what it writes
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The dialog shows every decision's answer as pills with a badge for where it came from —
        "tagged", "from the list", "by rule", "unanswered" (the one worth seeing) — and the group
        that claimed the row. Clicking an answer TAGS it: the row's label maps to the ref in the
        `tags` block; clicking the taken pill again hands the decision back to the rules. Every
        change goes through `writeTags`: the text above the banner "# Answers taken by hand.
        Written by the ranking view — keep last." is kept byte-for-byte, comments included, and
        only the block from the banner down is rewritten, then written straight through the
        configured writer. The view re-parses its own text and re-ranks at once. A row with no
        label cannot be tagged. "Reload" re-reads this file, the decisions and the list.
  - key: applied-elsewhere
    label: Another kind applies it
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Elsewhere
    detail: A policy is rules; the data lives in the documents that name it.
    content:
      key: elsewhere
      label: Who applies a policy
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Who applies a policy
        description: The same engines, called from other kinds.
        sections:
          - title: A data view
            body: |
              A `.jsonl` names a table policy in `$policy`; the composed view reads it live, keeps,
              sorts, cuts and picks columns with `applyTablePolicy`, reports its problems in the
              strip, and re-reads it on `studio:fs-changed`. `studio-check --collect` applies it
              the same way.
          - title: A journey
            body: |
              A stage's `fanout: {run: chain.policy, ranks: [1, 3]}` fans a pool into per-item
              columns by a run's ranks; a lane's `answers:` evaluates a run's chain for one row and
              shows every visible dimension's answer and the rank it lands. A policy step with a
              mini journey opens that journey as its explanation.
          - title: A memory
            body: |
              A `.memory`'s `decisions` block is the ranking's decisions block verbatim — the same
              `derive` rules and clause engine decide which focus a unit falls under.
          - title: A collection
            body: |
              A `.collection` is where a policy comes FROM: its decisions log the reasons a later
              policy is written to reproduce.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: A file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Using
    detail: This file re-parses and the role is picked again; a file it names is re-read only on Reload or reopen.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: What is re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text, and the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error. The file re-parses and its role is picked again —
        a run or a ranking then reads its policy and list afresh, an order policy its tags
        policy. A change to a NAMED file — the list, the tags policy, the order policy — is not
        watched: "Reload" (run, ranking) or reopening picks it up. The bucket machine's applied
        items, the order view's combination and the tags view's highlight are state and stay.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — the table role, the bucket machine and a run each have a problem list; a tags or order policy passes when its parser does.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: The order it runs in, the summary line, and what it leaves alone.
        sections:
          - title: In order
            body: |
              1. Parse the YAML — a parse error is the only problem reported.
              2. `role: table` → `parsePolicyKindFile` and its problems; summary `table policy: N
                 filters, N sort keys, N columns, first N`.
              3. `role: tags` or `role: order` → `parsePolicyKindFile`; summary `tags policy` /
                 `order policy`.
              4. Anything else → `policyProblems`: the field table's required rows and every
                 fallback the lenient parser takes. For the bucket machine: no `title`; a param with
                 no `key`, a duplicate key, or a `type` outside string / number / boolean; a bucket
                 with no `key`, a duplicate, or a `start`/`end` that is not `HH:MM`; a `default`
                 that is no declared bucket; no `cases`; a case with no `bucket`, a bucket no
                 `buckets:` entry declares (when any is), no `when` (write `when: []` to say
                 "always"); a clause with no `param`, a param `params:` does not declare (when any
                 is), no `op`, an `op` outside the vocabulary — each of these drops the clause, and
                 a case with no clauses left matches EVERYTHING, so the line says so — or a `value`
                 the op cannot use: `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` without a number, `between` without
                 `[low, high]`, `in`/`not_in` without a list, `is_*`/`*_empty` with one, a text op
                 without one. For a run: a `map` entry that is not a field name or a list of them,
                 a `date` that is not `YYYY-MM-DD`. `policy:` without `items:` (or the reverse) is
                 named as a run missing half of itself.
              5. The summary line: `N params, N cases, N buckets, default x` for the bucket
                 machine, `run: <policy> over <items>, N params mapped` for a run. The page and
                 the endpoint do the same.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              A run's or a ranking's `items`, a run's `policy`, an order's `tags` — none is read;
              they are files beside the policy. A derive rule's unknown op in a tags policy (the
              clause is dropped). A group's ref naming a decision or a value that does not exist
              (it never holds). Whether the roles chain to each other.
  - key: authored
    label: A policy is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a policy
        description: From the template to rules that run — the role decides the shape.
        decisions:
          - key: want
            label: What should it do?
            values:
              - { key: sort, label: Sort inputs into buckets }
              - { key: rank, label: Rank a list of rows }
              - { key: rows, label: Filter and order data rows }
        steps:
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template policy > new.policy` — the bucket machine, with one param,
              two buckets, a default and one case. Every other role starts from it by replacing
              the body.
          - key: switch
            label: Write the params, the buckets, then the cases in order
            after: [template]
            when: [want=sort]
            detail: |
              Declare every param the clauses read, every bucket a case names, and the cases from
              most specific to least — the first match wins and stops. Give buckets `start` and
              `end` when a run should draw a day.
          - key: chain
            label: Split the criteria from the judgement
            after: [template]
            when: [want=rank]
            detail: |
              A tags policy (`role: tags`) with one dimension per fact and ordered derive rules,
              a default rule last; then an order policy (`role: order`) naming it in `tags`, its
              entries best first, and a catch-all `{}` at the bottom so nothing is parked.
          - key: run
            label: Write the run
            after: [chain, switch]
            when: [want=rank]
            detail: |
              `policy:` the order (or bucket) policy, `items:` the list, `map:` param → the row's
              columns (`label` for its label). Open it and click rows for why.
          - key: table
            label: Write the table rules
            after: [template]
            when: [want=rows]
            detail: |
              `role: table`, `where` with `field` and `op`, `sort` with `dir`, `columns` with `"*"`
              for the rest, `limit`. No source, no labels — they belong to the `.jsonl` that names
              this file in `$policy`.
          - key: check
            label: Check it
            after: [switch, run, table]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.policy` — a bucket policy, a run and a table policy each print
              their problems and a summary line; a misspelled `op`, an undeclared param or bucket,
              a case without a bucket are each one line naming the case and clause. A tags or
              order policy passes whenever the YAML does, so open it and pick a combination or
              click a row.
